# <%= title %>

A [Sidereal](https://github.com/ismasan/sidereal) app.

## Run

```sh
bundle install
<% if database? -%>
bin/sid db migrations run
<% end -%>
bin/dev
```

Then open <http://localhost:<%= port %>>. `PORT`, `HOST` and `COUNT` (worker processes) can be set in the environment.
<% if database? -%>

The migrations in `db/migrations/` are committed and `storage/` isn't, so `bin/sid db migrations run` is how a checkout gets its database — copy one over instead if you'd rather keep the data.
<% end -%>

`bin/dev` runs `bundle exec falcon host` and reloads the app whenever a Ruby file changes. Reloading needs a file watcher: [watchexec](https://github.com/watchexec/watchexec) (`brew install watchexec`) or fswatch. Without one, the server still runs. In production, run `bundle exec falcon host`. The app derives the keys for its session cookies and for signing the URLs of pages' live updates from one secret: `SIDEREAL_SECRET`, or else `storage/secret.txt`, which the first boot creates. On one machine whose `storage/` survives deploys, that's all it needs. On several machines, or a disk that a deploy replaces, set `SIDEREAL_SECRET` to a random string of at least 64 bytes (`bin/sid secret` makes one), the same in every process and across deploys. Changing the secret logs everyone out.
<% if rspec? -%>

## Test

```sh
bundle exec rspec
```

`spec/web/ui/welcome_page_spec.rb` tests the command the welcome page accepts with `Sidereal::Testing::Harness`, which `spec/spec_helper.rb` requires. It handles a page's or component's commands as `App` does, posted as a form would post them, and returns what each handler did: what it dispatched, what it patched into the page, and the errors it rejected the command with.
<% end -%>

